L = {}
L["jordan"] = """Moses was dead. He had led Israel for forty years in the desert, and he died on Mount Nebo, within sight of the Promised Land, but without entering it. God had chosen a new leader, Joshua, who had been Moses' helper since the Exodus. God told him, Moses my servant is dead. Now then, you and all these people, get ready to cross the Jordan River into the land I am about to give to them. Be strong and courageous. Do not be afraid, for the Lord your God will be with you wherever you go.

It was the time of the spring harvest, when the Jordan River is at flood stage and overflows its banks. This was not a small stream. It would have been very deep, fast, and dangerous. And there was no bridge. The people of Israel stood on one side of the river, with the land God had promised them on the other.

Joshua sent two spies ahead to Jericho. They stayed in the house of a woman named Rahab, who hid them and told them that the people of the land were terrified of Israel. Then they came back with good news. The Lord has surely given the whole land into our hands. Everyone who lives in the country is melting in fear because of us.

Early in the morning, Joshua and all the Israelites moved out and camped by the Jordan. After three days, the officers went through the camp and gave orders. When you see the ark of the covenant of the Lord your God, and the Levitical priests carrying it, you are to move out from your positions and follow it. Then you will know which way to go, since you have not been this way before. But keep a distance of about a thousand yards between you and the ark. Do not go near it.

Joshua told the people to consecrate themselves, for tomorrow the Lord will do amazing things among you. Then he told the priests, take up the ark of the covenant and pass on ahead of the people. So they took it up and went ahead of them.

The Lord told Joshua, today I will begin to exalt you in the eyes of all Israel, so they may know that I am with you as I was with Moses. Tell the priests who carry the ark to go and stand in the Jordan.

Here is the amazing part. The water did not part until the priests stepped into it. As soon as the priests who carried the ark reached the Jordan and their feet touched the water's edge, the water from upstream stopped flowing. It piled up in a heap a great distance away, at a town called Adam. The water flowing down to the Dead Sea was completely cut off. So the people crossed over opposite Jericho.

The priests who carried the ark of the covenant stood on dry ground in the middle of the Jordan, while all Israel passed by until the whole nation had crossed on dry ground. They did not cross with the river bottom still wet. It was dry land, like it was at the Red Sea.

Then Joshua told twelve men, one from each tribe, to take twelve stones from the middle of the riverbed and carry them to the camp. They set them up as a memorial at Gilgal. In the future, when your children ask you, what do these stones mean? Tell them that the flow of the Jordan was cut off before the ark of the covenant of the Lord. These stones are to be a memorial to the people of Israel forever.

Why does this matter? This was Israel's second great crossing. Their parents crossed the Red Sea when they left. Now the children crossed the Jordan when they came home. It showed that the God who had been with Moses was with Joshua, and that the miracles hadn't ended.

It also shows how God often works. The water didn't part until the priests' feet were wet. They had to step out in faith first. God does not usually show the path all at once. He shows it one step at a time.

What happened after? On the other side, the manna stopped. The people ate the food of the land for the first time. And Joshua met a man with a drawn sword who said he was the commander of the Lord's army. The conquest of Canaan began with Jericho.

What does this mean for us today? We often want to see the river part before we step in. This story says to step in first. Obedience comes before the miracle. God asked the priests to walk into a flooded river, carrying something heavy, and the water stopped as soon as they got their feet wet.

It also reminds us to build memorials. Joshua piled up stones so that the next generation would ask what they meant. We need to remember what God has done, and tell it to our children.

That is why this story is so important. Israel entered the Promised Land with a miracle, and they did it behind the ark, which stood for the presence of God. We too go forward into the unknown not because we can see the way, but because the one we follow has already gone ahead."""

L["jericho"] = """Jericho was the first city Israel faced in the Promised Land. It was a fortress, and one of the oldest cities in the world. The Bible says its gates were tightly shut because of the Israelites. No one went out and no one came in. The walls were thick and strong, and Israel had no siege weapons, no ladders, and no army trained for this.

Joshua was thinking about how to attack when he saw a man standing in front of him with a drawn sword in his hand. Joshua walked up to him and asked, are you for us or for our enemies? Neither, he replied. But as commander of the army of the Lord I have now come. Joshua fell facedown to the ground in reverence. The commander said, take off your sandals, for the place where you are standing is holy. This was the same thing God said to Moses at the burning bush. It reminded Joshua that this was not his battle. It was the Lord's.

God gave Joshua the strangest battle plan in history. March around the city once with all the armed men. Do this for six days. Have seven priests carry trumpets of rams' horns in front of the ark. On the seventh day, march around the city seven times, with the priests blowing the trumpets. When you hear them sound a long blast, have the whole army give a loud shout. Then the wall of the city will collapse, and the army will go up, everyone straight in.

Joshua told the people to keep quiet. Do not give a war cry, do not raise your voices, do not say a word until the day I tell you to shout. Then shout! So for six days, the whole army walked around the city, and nobody said anything, just the trumpets and the footsteps. Imagine being a soldier on those walls, watching a silent army circle the city every day. Imagine being an Israelite, walking around a city without fighting.

On the seventh day, they got up at daybreak and marched around the city seven times. The seventh time around, when the priests sounded the trumpet blast, Joshua commanded the army, shout! For the Lord has given you the city. The Bible says that when the trumpets sounded and the people shouted, the wall collapsed. Everyone charged straight in, and they took the city.

Joshua had given a command about the city. It was devoted to the Lord, and the Israelites were not to take anything from it. But there was one exception. Rahab the prostitute, and everyone with her in her house, were to be spared, because she hid the spies. Joshua sent the two spies to bring her out. She and her family were saved, and the Bible says she has lived among the Israelites to this day.

Rahab shows up again in a surprising place. Matthew's gospel lists her in the family line of Jesus. She married Salmon, and their son was Boaz, who married Ruth, and from them came King David. A woman from Jericho became an ancestor of the Messiah. Hebrews also lists her in its hall of faith. By faith the walls of Jericho fell, after the army had marched around them for seven days. By faith the prostitute Rahab, because she welcomed the spies, was not killed with those who were disobedient.

Why does this matter? Jericho shows that victory comes from God and not from human strength. The Israelites didn't do anything that would make a wall fall. All they did was obey, even when it made no sense. The battle was won before a single sword was drawn.

It also shows that the story of Israel was never only for Israel. Rahab was an outsider who believed what she heard about God and risked her life. God honored it.

What happened after? Jericho was the first city, and more were to come. But soon after, Israel was defeated at the small city of Ai, because a man named Achan had taken some of the forbidden treasure from Jericho. Victory came from obedience, and so did defeat.

What does this mean for us today? Sometimes God's plan doesn't seem to make sense. Marching in circles will not win a war. But the plan he gives us is often built to make sure we know who deserves the credit. When we do what God says, even when it looks foolish, we find that the wall comes down.

It also means that no one is too far gone. Rahab had a hard past, and God used her. Whatever your past has been, it doesn't decide where your story goes.

That is why this story is so important. It teaches that faith is not a feeling. It is walking around a wall for seven days because God said to. And it reminds us that God keeps his promises, and that the walls that look the strongest are not strong enough to stop him."""

L["sun-stands"] = """After Jericho and Ai, Israel kept moving into the land. News of what was happening spread quickly. One of the cities nearby, Gibeon, was afraid. The people of Gibeon tricked Joshua into making a treaty with them. They pretended to come from a far country, and wore worn-out clothes and carried moldy bread. Joshua and the leaders didn't ask the Lord. They made a peace treaty, and only found out three days later that Gibeon was a close neighbor. Because they had sworn an oath, Israel kept it.

When the other kings in the area heard that Gibeon had made peace with Israel, they were furious. Five Amorite kings joined their armies together and attacked Gibeon. The Gibeonites sent a message to Joshua at Gilgal. Do not abandon your servants. Come up to us quickly and save us! Help us, because all the Amorite kings from the hill country have joined forces against us.

Joshua could have said, you tricked us. Instead he kept his promise. He marched all night from Gilgal with his whole army, a steep climb of about twenty miles. The Lord said to Joshua, do not be afraid of them. I have given them into your hand. Not one of them will be able to withstand you. Joshua took them by surprise, and the Lord threw the enemy into confusion. Israel defeated them at Gibeon, and chased them along the road up to Beth Horon.

As they fled, the Lord hurled large hailstones down on them from the sky. More of them died from the hailstones than were killed by the swords of the Israelites. God was fighting for Israel, and the weather was part of the battle.

Then Joshua did something that no one had ever done before. On the day the Lord gave the Amorites over to Israel, Joshua said to the Lord in the presence of Israel, sun, stand still over Gibeon, and moon, over the Valley of Aijalon. So the sun stood still, and the moon stopped, till the nation avenged itself on its enemies.

The sun stopped in the middle of the sky and delayed going down about a full day. The Bible says, there has never been a day like it before or since, a day when the Lord listened to a human being. Surely the Lord was fighting for Israel!

People have wondered for centuries exactly what happened. Some believe the Earth's rotation actually slowed or stopped. Others think the light was prolonged in a way we can't explain. The Bible doesn't say how God did it. It just says that he did, and that it was an answer to the prayer of a man who had faith.

The five kings had hidden in a cave at Makkedah. Joshua had them brought out, and he told his commanders to put their feet on the necks of the kings. Do not be afraid, do not be discouraged. Be strong and courageous. This is what the Lord will do to all the enemies you are going to fight. Then Joshua went on to take the southern cities of Canaan.

Why does this matter? It shows that God defends his people and keeps his promises, and that he listens to prayer. It also shows that when you make a promise, you keep it. Joshua had been tricked into the treaty, but he honored it, and God backed him.

It also shows what a difference a leader's trust makes. Joshua had seen the Red Sea part as a young man, and he had seen the Jordan stop. He believed that if God said to go, God would win the battle. So he prayed boldly, and asked for something huge.

What happened after? Joshua kept on defeating the kings of the land, and eventually Israel got its share. Then Joshua gave the land to the tribes. At the end of his life he gave a final speech. As for me and my household, we will serve the Lord.

What does this mean for us today? Maybe you don't pray for the sun to stand still. But this story invites us to pray big prayers. Joshua asked for something impossible, and God did it. Do not be afraid to ask for large things, in line with what God has promised.

It also says to keep your word, even when keeping it is costly. A promise made in the name of the Lord is a serious thing. It's easy to want to get out of it when it costs us. Joshua kept it, and God honored him.

That is why this story is so important. It reminds us that the God who made the sun is in charge of it. Nothing is too large for him to move, and nothing in our lives is too small for him to hear."""

L["gideon"] = """After Joshua died, Israel went through a cycle again and again in the book of Judges. The people turned away from God and followed other gods. God let their enemies conquer them. The people cried out, and God sent a judge to rescue them. Then it began again. This time it was the Midianites. For seven years they raided Israel every harvest. They came with camels and flocks like swarms of locusts and left nothing behind. The Israelites hid in caves and mountain strongholds.

Gideon was threshing wheat in a winepress, to hide it from the Midianites. The angel of the Lord appeared to him and said, the Lord is with you, mighty warrior. Gideon was not feeling mighty. He said, pardon me, my lord, but if the Lord is with us, why has all this happened to us? Where are all his wonders? He told the angel that his clan was the weakest in Manasseh, and he was the least in his family. The Lord answered, I will be with you, and you will strike down all the Midianites.

Gideon asked for a sign. He put a wool fleece on the threshing floor and said, if there is dew only on the fleece and all the ground is dry, then I will know that you will save Israel by my hand. In the morning the fleece was soaked, and Gideon wrung out a bowl full of water. Then he asked again. This time, make the fleece dry and the ground wet. And that is what happened. God was patient with his doubts.

Gideon gathered an army of 32,000 men. But the Lord said, you have too many men. In order that Israel may not boast against me that her own strength has saved her, announce to the army, anyone who trembles with fear may turn back. Twenty-two thousand left, leaving 10,000. The Lord said, there are still too many. Take them down to the water. Those who lap the water with their tongues, like dogs, put to one side. Only 300 men did. The Lord said, with the 300 men that lapped I will save you and give the Midianites into your hands.

The Midianite camp was as thick as a swarm of locusts, 135,000 men. Gideon had only 300. In the night, God told him to go down to the camp and listen. If you are afraid, take your servant. He heard a Midianite telling a dream about a loaf of barley bread that tumbled into the camp and flattened the tent. His friend answered, this can be nothing other than the sword of Gideon. God has given the Midianites into his hands. Gideon bowed down and worshiped.

He divided the 300 men into three companies. He gave each man a trumpet and an empty jar with a torch inside. Watch me, he told them. Follow my lead. When I and everyone with me blow our trumpets, then from all around the camp blow yours and shout, for the Lord and for Gideon!

They arrived at the edge of the camp at the beginning of the middle watch. They blew their trumpets and smashed the jars. All three companies blew the trumpets and broke the jars. They held the torches in their left hands and the trumpets in their right, and shouted, a sword for the Lord and for Gideon! While every man held his position, all the Midianites ran, crying out as they fled. The Lord caused the men throughout the camp to turn on each other with their swords.

Why does this matter? God picks the weak on purpose, so that no one can take the credit. Gideon began as a frightened man in a winepress, and God called him a mighty warrior before he showed any sign of it. God saw what Gideon would become and spoke to that.

It also shows how God deals with our doubts. He didn't rebuke Gideon for asking for the fleece twice. He gave him what he needed to believe. But in the end he asked Gideon to trust him with a battle plan that made no sense.

What happened after? Israel had peace for forty years. They wanted to make Gideon king, but he refused. The Lord will rule over you. Sadly, he made a gold ephod from the plunder, and the people worshiped it. Even a great man can fail after a great victory.

What does this mean for us today? If you feel small, inadequate, or hidden, so did Gideon. God doesn't wait for us to feel ready. He meets us in the winepress and tells us who we are in his eyes.

It also tells us that numbers don't win battles. Three hundred men with torches and jars, and the Lord in front. God likes to work with fewer resources, so that it is obvious it was him.

That is why this story is so important. It reminds us that the God who works through the weak, through jars of clay as Paul puts it, can use anyone. The light is in us, and the jars have to break for it to show."""

L["samson-lion"] = """Israel had again turned away from God, and this time he let the Philistines rule them for forty years. Before Samson was born, an angel appeared to his mother, who had been unable to have children. The angel told her she would have a son who would begin to deliver Israel from the Philistines. The boy was to be a Nazirite from birth. This meant three things. He would never drink wine, he would never touch anything dead or unclean, and no razor would ever touch his head. His long hair was the sign of his promise to God.

Samson grew up, and the Lord blessed him. The Spirit of the Lord began to stir him. He was given enormous physical strength. But he was also headstrong, and he often let his desires lead him instead of God.

One day Samson went down to Timnah, a Philistine town, and saw a young Philistine woman. He came back and told his parents, I have seen a Philistine woman in Timnah. Get her for me as my wife. His parents were upset. Isn't there an acceptable woman among your relatives? Why do you have to go to the uncircumcised Philistines? But Samson insisted. Get her for me. She's right for me. The Bible adds that his parents did not know that this was from the Lord, who was seeking an occasion to confront the Philistines.

As Samson went down to Timnah with his parents, a young lion came roaring toward him. The Spirit of the Lord came powerfully upon him, and he tore the lion apart with his bare hands as he might have torn a young goat. But he told neither his father nor his mother what he had done.

Some time later, when he went back to marry her, he turned aside to look at the lion's carcass. In it was a swarm of bees and some honey. He scooped out the honey with his hands and ate as he went along. He gave some to his parents, but he did not tell them that he had taken the honey from the lion's carcass. Honey from a dead lion. It was a secret thing, and a strange one, because a Nazirite was not supposed to touch anything dead.

At his wedding feast, Samson told the thirty young Philistine companions a riddle. Out of the eater, something to eat. Out of the strong, something sweet. If you can give me the answer within the seven days of the feast, I will give you thirty linen garments and thirty sets of clothes. If you can't, you must give me the same. They couldn't solve it, so they pressured Samson's wife. She wept and badgered him until on the seventh day, he told her. She told the Philistines. They answered, what is sweeter than honey? What is stronger than a lion?

Samson was furious. He went to Ashkelon, killed thirty Philistines, took their belongings, and gave their clothes to the men who had explained the riddle. Then in anger he went back to his father's house. His wife was given to his companion.

Why does this matter? It sets the scene for the rest of Samson's life, and shows both his strength and his weakness. God gave him power for a purpose, to start freeing Israel. But Samson used it again and again for personal revenge. He was like Israel itself, called by God and gifted by God but often doing what was right in his own eyes.

The riddle also has a deeper meaning. Out of death came sweetness. A dead lion had honey in it. God often brings life out of things that seem dead. And the greatest example is the cross.

What happened after? A cycle of revenge between Samson and the Philistines began. It led to the great story of Delilah, which is next. In the end, Samson did begin to deliver Israel, but at a terrible cost.

What does this mean for us today? Gifts are not the same as character. Samson had more strength than anyone in the Bible, and yet he could not control his temper or his desires. We need more than talents. We need to be led by the Spirit and not by our impulses.

It also says that God can use flawed people to do his work. He worked through Samson, not because of his choices but in spite of them. That should humble us, and encourage us. Hebrews lists Samson among the heroes of faith.

That is why this story is so important. It is a warning and a promise together. A life set apart for God can be wasted by small compromises that seem harmless, but God does not give up on the person who was set apart. The story of Samson is not over yet."""
